=== L2 Inverting and Non-Inverting Op-Amp Circuits ===
id: b8f50446-bc0c-45fa-a1d3-e741944db3da
slug: inverting-and-non-inverting-op-amp-circuits
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:47:36.709099+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 30

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Design inverting and non-inverting op-amp amplifiers and calculate gain from resistor ratios.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Operational Amplifier</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">An <dfn class="term" data-def="An integrated circuit with two inputs and one output, used as the building block of precision amplifier circuits.">operational amplifier</dfn> (op-amp) is an integrated circuit designed to amplify the difference between its two input voltages. It has an inverting input (marked &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo;), a non-inverting input (marked &ldquo;+&rdquo;), and a single output. Without external components, its open-loop gain is enormous &mdash; typically hundreds of thousands &mdash; which makes it unstable for most applications. In practice, resistors are connected around the op-amp to form a <dfn class="term" data-def="A connection from the output of an amplifier back to its inverting input, used to set a stable, precise gain.">negative feedback</dfn> loop that sets a stable, predictable gain.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n3">Two resistors define the gain in both configurations covered here: \(R_1\) (the input resistor, connected between the signal source and the op-amp input) and \(R_f\) (the feedback resistor, connected between the output and the inverting input). An important rule for your exam: both resistors must be at least 1 k&Omega; in any op-amp circuit design.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">The Inverting Amplifier</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">In an <dfn class="term" data-def="An op-amp circuit where the input signal connects to the inverting (-) input via R1 and the feedback resistor Rf connects from output to inverting input; the output is phase-inverted relative to the input.">inverting amplifier</dfn>, the input signal is applied through \(R_1\) to the &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo; input. The feedback resistor \(R_f\) connects from the output back to the same &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo; input. The &ldquo;+&rdquo; input is connected to 0 V (ground).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Because the signal enters the inverting input, the output is 180&deg; out of phase with the input &mdash; if the input goes positive, the output goes negative. The voltage gain is:</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the inverting amplifier gain formula from memory. Which resistor goes on top, and why does the gain have a negative sign?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Inverting amplifier voltage gain:</p>
  <p>$$G = -\frac{R_f}{R_1}$$</p>
  <p>The negative sign indicates phase inversion. Only the magnitude \(|G| = R_f / R_1\) determines how much the voltage is multiplied.</p>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n8">The concept of a <dfn class="term" data-def="The inverting input of an op-amp in a closed-loop circuit, which is held at approximately 0 V by the negative feedback even though it is not directly connected to ground.">virtual earth</dfn> helps explain why the formula works. Negative feedback forces the voltage at the inverting input to be essentially 0 V, even though that point is not connected directly to ground. This means that \(R_1\) and \(R_f\) share the same voltage reference at the inverting pin, and Kirchhoff&rsquo;s current law gives the gain ratio.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example: Inverting Amplifier Gain</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">An inverting amplifier uses \(R_1 = 10\) k&Omega; and \(R_f = 100\) k&Omega;. The input signal is a sine wave with a peak of 50 mV.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n10">Step 1 &mdash; Calculate gain: \(G = -R_f / R_1 = -100 / 10 = -10\).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Step 2 &mdash; Calculate output peak: \(V_{OUT} = G \times V_{IN} = -10 \times 50\text{ mV} = -500\text{ mV}\).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n12">The magnitude is 500 mV (0.5 V peak) and the output is inverted relative to the input. On a voltage&ndash;time graph, when the input sine wave is at its positive peak, the output is at its negative peak.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n13">If you need to find \(R_f\) for a target gain: \(R_f = |G| \times R_1 = 10 \times 10 = 100\) k&Omega;. Always check that both resistors are &ge; 1 k&Omega;.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">The Non-Inverting Amplifier</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">In a <dfn class="term" data-def="An op-amp circuit where the input signal connects to the non-inverting (+) input; the output is in phase with the input and the gain is always greater than 1.">non-inverting amplifier</dfn>, the input signal connects to the &ldquo;+&rdquo; input. The feedback network (\(R_f\) and \(R_1\)) connects from the output back to the &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo; input, with \(R_1\) going from the inverting input to ground. The output is in phase with the input &mdash; positive input gives positive output.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Close this and write the non-inverting gain formula from memory. What is the minimum possible gain, and why can it never be zero?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Non-inverting amplifier voltage gain:</p>
  <p>$$G = 1 + \frac{R_f}{R_1}$$</p>
  <p>The &ldquo;1 +&rdquo; means the minimum gain is always greater than 1 (since \(R_f / R_1\) is positive). There is no phase inversion.</p>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n17">The non-inverting configuration is one of the least well answered topics in recent exams. The key distinction from the inverting case is: the input goes to the &ldquo;+&rdquo; pin and the feedback still goes to the &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo; pin via \(R_f\). If you draw the circuit, \(R_1\) sits between the &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo; pin and ground, and \(R_f\) connects the output back to that same &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo; pin.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example: Non-Inverting Amplifier Design</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Design a non-inverting amplifier with a gain of 11. Choose \(R_1 = 10\) k&Omega;.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n19">Rearrange: \(R_f = (G - 1) \times R_1 = (11 - 1) \times 10 = 100\) k&Omega;.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n20">Check: \(G = 1 + R_f / R_1 = 1 + 100/10 = 1 + 10 = 11\). Correct. Both resistors are above 1 k&Omega;.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n21">With \(V_{IN} = 100\) mV: \(V_{OUT} = 11 \times 0.1 = 1.1\) V. The output is in phase with the input (positive input gives positive output).</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n22">Comparing the Two Configurations</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n23">The table below summarises the key differences between the two op-amp circuits:</p>
<table data-narration-id="n24">
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Inverting</th><th>Non-inverting</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Input connects to</td><td>&minus; pin (via R1)</td><td>+ pin</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Gain formula</td><td>\(-R_f / R_1\)</td><td>\(1 + R_f / R_1\)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Phase shift</td><td>180&deg; inversion</td><td>No inversion</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Minimum gain</td><td>Can be &lt;1</td><td>Always &ge;1</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p data-narration-id="n25">In both circuits, the output cannot exceed the supply rail voltage. Always check for clipping when the calculated output is close to the supply voltage.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n27">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n28">Inverting amplifier: input to the &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo; pin via \(R_1\), feedback via \(R_f\); gain \(G = -R_f / R_1\); output is phase-inverted.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">Non-inverting amplifier: input to the &ldquo;+&rdquo; pin; gain \(G = 1 + R_f / R_1\); output is in phase; minimum gain is always &gt; 1.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n30">Both resistors must be &ge; 1 k&Omega; in exam design questions; always check the output against supply rails to identify clipping.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n26">The non-inverting amplifier has been cited as the least-successfully answered question in recent exams. Practise drawing it from memory: signal to the &ldquo;+&rdquo; pin, \(R_1\) from the &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo; pin to ground, \(R_f\) from output back to the &ldquo;&minus;&rdquo; pin. Do not drop the negative sign from the inverting gain formula &mdash; the examiner expects to see \(G = -R_f / R_1\) and will penalise a positive answer. Both resistors must be &ge; 1 k&Omega; in any design question.</p>

