=== L5 555 Astable Oscillators and Mark-Space Ratio ===
id: 1742666e-eb3a-4f41-b044-5b22950a7132
slug: 555-astable-oscillators-and-mark-space-ratio
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:23:19.324396+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 43

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Using a 555 timer in astable mode: calculating frequency, period and mark-space ratio.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">From One-Shot to Continuous Oscillation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The previous lesson showed how the 555 timer in monostable mode fires a single timed pulse when triggered. In <dfn class="term" data-def="A 555 timer operating mode that oscillates continuously without any trigger, producing a repeating square-wave output.">astable</dfn> mode, there is no stable state at all &mdash; the output switches continuously between HIGH and LOW, producing a repeating waveform without any external trigger. This makes the 555 astable a simple and reliable <dfn class="term" data-def="A circuit that produces a continuously repeating electrical signal, usually a square or rectangular wave.">oscillator</dfn>, used in LED flashers, alarm sounders, clock pulse generators and many other applications.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">The Astable Circuit</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The 555 astable uses two external resistors (\(R_1\) and \(R_2\)) and one capacitor (\(C\)). The capacitor charges through both \(R_1\) and \(R_2\) in series, but discharges through only \(R_2\). This asymmetry means the time the output spends HIGH is longer than the time it spends LOW, producing a waveform where the mark time and space time are different.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n5">The timing equations for the two half-cycles are:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n6"><strong>Time HIGH</strong> (mark time): \(T_{ON} = 0.7 \times (R_1 + R_2) \times C\)</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Time LOW</strong> (space time): \(T_{OFF} = 0.7 \times R_2 \times C\)</li>
</ul>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Period and Frequency</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The total period of one complete cycle is the sum of the two half-cycles: \(T = T_{ON} + T_{OFF}\). Substituting the expressions above:</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the 555 astable frequency formula from memory. Identify which multiplier belongs to which resistor.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>555 astable frequency:</p>
  <p>$$f = \frac{1.44}{(R_1 + 2R_2) \times C}$$</p>
  <p>where \(f\) is in hertz, \(R_1\) and \(R_2\) are in ohms, and \(C\) is in farads. Note that \(R_2\) is multiplied by 2, not \(R_1\).</p>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n11">Period \(T = 1/f\). You can also find the period directly:</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">$$T = 0.7 \times (R_1 + 2R_2) \times C$$</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Mark-Space Ratio</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14"><dfn class="term" data-def="The ratio of the time the output is HIGH (mark) to the time the output is LOW (space) in a repeating waveform.">Mark-space ratio</dfn> (sometimes called mark-to-space ratio) describes the shape of the output waveform:</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">$$\text{Mark-space ratio} = \frac{T_{ON}}{T_{OFF}} = \frac{R_1 + R_2}{R_2}$$</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Because the capacitor charges through both \(R_1\) and \(R_2\) but discharges through only \(R_2\), \(T_{ON}\) is always greater than \(T_{OFF}\) when \(R_1 > 0\). This means the mark-space ratio is always greater than 1 in a standard 555 astable. A mark-space ratio of 1:1 (equal HIGH and LOW times) is called a 50% <dfn class="term" data-def="The fraction of one complete cycle during which the output is HIGH, expressed as a percentage.">duty cycle</dfn>. The standard 555 astable cannot achieve exactly 50% duty cycle unless \(R_1 = 0\) &Omega; (which would damage the IC), so a modified circuit using a diode is needed for a 1:1 ratio.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example: 555 Astable Calculation with Unit Multipliers</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">A 555 astable has \(R_1 = 10\) k&Omega;, \(R_2 = 47\) k&Omega; and \(C = 100\) nF. Calculate the frequency and mark-space ratio.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Step 1 &mdash; Convert units to SI base units:</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n19">\(R_1 = 10 \times 10^{3} = 10{,}000\) &Omega;; \(R_2 = 47 \times 10^{3} = 47{,}000\) &Omega;; \(C = 100 \times 10^{-9}\) F.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n20">Step 2 &mdash; Calculate the denominator:</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n21">\(R_1 + 2R_2 = 10{,}000 + 2 \times 47{,}000 = 10{,}000 + 94{,}000 = 104{,}000\) &Omega;</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n22">Step 3 &mdash; Substitute into the frequency formula:</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n23">$$f = \frac{1.44}{104{,}000 \times 100 \times 10^{-9}} = \frac{1.44}{104{,}000 \times 10^{-7}} = \frac{1.44}{0.0104} \approx 138\text{ Hz}$$</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n24">Step 4 &mdash; Mark-space ratio:</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n25">$$\text{Mark-space ratio} = \frac{R_1 + R_2}{R_2} = \frac{10{,}000 + 47{,}000}{47{,}000} = \frac{57{,}000}{47{,}000} \approx 1.21$$</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n26">The output is HIGH for about 1.21 times as long as it is LOW.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n27">Reading an Oscilloscope Trace</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n28">An oscilloscope displays voltage on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal axis. Two key controls determine the scale: <dfn class="term" data-def="The voltage represented by each vertical division on the oscilloscope screen, set by the volts/div control.">volts per division</dfn> (V/div) and <dfn class="term" data-def="The time represented by each horizontal division on the oscilloscope screen, set by the time/div or timebase control.">time per division</dfn> (time/div or timebase).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n29">To read a 555 astable output waveform from an oscilloscope trace:</p>
<ol>
  <li data-narration-id="n30"><strong>Amplitude:</strong> Count the number of vertical divisions from the bottom of the waveform to the top of the HIGH level. Multiply by V/div. This is the amplitude of the waveform. Do not confuse amplitude with peak-to-peak voltage &mdash; for a waveform that swings between 0 V and +9 V, the amplitude is 9 V and the peak-to-peak voltage is also 9 V (but for a waveform centred on 0 V, peak-to-peak is twice the amplitude).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n31"><strong>Period:</strong> Count the number of horizontal divisions for one complete cycle (one HIGH + one LOW). Multiply by time/div. This gives the period \(T\) in seconds (or ms or &mu;s, depending on the timebase setting).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n32"><strong>Frequency:</strong> \(f = 1/T\). If \(T\) was measured in ms, convert to seconds before dividing: \(1\) ms \(= 10^{-3}\) s.</li>
</ol>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n33" data-revision-tip="Test yourself: what is the common exam error when measuring amplitude from an oscilloscope? Write the correct definition of amplitude and peak-to-peak voltage in your own words.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Amplitude is measured in volts (V), <strong>not milliseconds</strong>. Period is measured in time (s, ms, or &mu;s), <strong>not volts</strong>. These unit errors are the most common mistake in oscilloscope questions. Always state the V/div and time/div values before reading off measurements.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Exam Errors in 555 Astable Questions</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n34">Examiner reports identify 555 astable questions as among the worst-performing on both papers. The most frequent mistakes are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li data-narration-id="n35">Mishandling the k and &mu; multipliers: \(R = 47\) k&Omega; must be entered as \(47 \times 10^3\) or \(47{,}000\) &Omega; in the formula &mdash; not as \(47\).</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n36">Multiplying \(R_1\) by 2 instead of \(R_2\): the formula is \(R_1 + 2R_2\), so \(R_2\) gets the factor of 2, not \(R_1\).</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n37">Giving amplitude in ms or period in V &mdash; this is a category error. Amplitude is always in volts; period is always in time units.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n38">Confusing peak-to-peak and amplitude for waveforms that swing symmetrically around 0 V.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n40">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n41">The 555 astable oscillates continuously; frequency is \(f = 1.44 / [(R_1 + 2R_2)C]\) where \(R_2\) carries the factor of 2.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n42">Mark-space ratio \(= T_{ON} / T_{OFF} = (R_1 + R_2) / R_2\); it is always &gt; 1 in a standard astable and equals the duty cycle above 50%.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n43">On an oscilloscope, amplitude is read in volts (vertical divisions &times; V/div) and period in time (horizontal divisions &times; time/div) &mdash; never swap the units.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n39">In the frequency formula \(f = 1.44 / [(R_1 + 2R_2)C]\), note that \(R_2\) is multiplied by 2 &mdash; not \(R_1\). Always convert resistors to ohms and capacitors to farads before substituting. On oscilloscope questions, state the time/div before reading the period, and the V/div before reading the amplitude &mdash; then give amplitude in volts and period in seconds or milliseconds (never the wrong unit for the wrong quantity).</p>

