=== L6 D-Type Flip-Flops, Latches and Up-Counters ===
id: d80d1b60-25a2-412e-af77-5eb34eb4f1e5
slug: d-type-flip-flops-latches-and-up-counters
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-15T21:15:42.606846+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 33

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How rising-edge-triggered D-type flip-flops work as data latches and 1-bit and 2-bit binary counters.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Storing a Single Bit</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Logic gates process information instantly &mdash; their output changes as soon as their inputs change. But digital systems also need to <em>store</em> information: a counter must remember its current count between clock pulses, and a memory circuit must hold data after the input is removed. The simplest storage element in digital electronics is the <dfn class="term" data-def="A digital circuit that can store one bit of data; its output retains its last state until a clock edge causes it to sample its D input.">D-type flip-flop</dfn>.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">The D-Type Flip-Flop</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">A D-type flip-flop has three inputs &mdash; D (data), CLK (clock) &mdash; and two complementary outputs, Q and \(\overline{Q}\) (Q-bar). The key property is that it is <dfn class="term" data-def="A flip-flop that only changes its outputs at the moment a clock signal transitions from LOW to HIGH (the rising edge), ignoring the D input at all other times.">rising-edge triggered</dfn>: the flip-flop samples the D input <em>only</em> at the instant the clock signal transitions from LOW to HIGH. At all other times, changes on D have no effect.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the D-type rule in one sentence from memory: what happens at the rising edge, and what happens between clock edges?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>At every rising clock edge: Q copies the value of D. Between rising edges: Q holds its stored value regardless of what D does. \(\overline{Q}\) is always the logical opposite of Q.</p>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n6">In symbol form, the D-type is drawn as a rectangle with D and CLK on the left, Q on the right, and \(\overline{Q}\) on the right with a small circle (bubble) to indicate inversion. The clock input is marked with a small triangle to indicate edge-triggering.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Timing Diagram for a D-Type Flip-Flop</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">A timing diagram shows the D, CLK, Q and \(\overline{Q}\) signals plotted against time. Reading one systematically is a core exam skill.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Consider the following sequence (read each rising clock edge left to right):</p>

<table data-narration-id="n10">
  <thead><tr><th>Rising edge</th><th>D at that moment</th><th>Q after edge</th><th>Q-bar after edge</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>1st</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2nd</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3rd</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4th</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Between any two consecutive rising edges, Q stays constant &mdash; even if D changes. This is the &ldquo;latch&rdquo; behaviour: the flip-flop has stored the bit from the previous rising edge and holds it until the next one.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">The D-Type as a Data Latch</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">When a D-type flip-flop is used to hold a data bit so it can be read later, it is described as a <dfn class="term" data-def="A D-type flip-flop used to store a single bit of data so that it remains available at the output after the input has changed.">data latch</dfn>. The flip-flop &ldquo;latches on&rdquo; to the value of D at the rising clock edge and holds it. This is useful when a sensor produces a brief pulse that would otherwise be lost before a processor can act on it.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Binary Counters from D-Type Flip-Flops</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">If the \(\overline{Q}\) output of a D-type flip-flop is connected back to its own D input, the circuit divides the input clock frequency by 2. Each rising clock edge causes the flip-flop to toggle &mdash; the output alternates between 0 and 1 every clock cycle. This is a 1-bit <dfn class="term" data-def="A circuit built from flip-flops that counts upward in binary, advancing by one step on each clock pulse.">binary up-counter</dfn>.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain: how do you connect a D-type flip-flop to make it divide the clock frequency by 2? Write the connection from memory.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>To make a 1-bit binary counter: connect \(\overline{Q}\) back to D. Each rising clock edge causes Q to toggle. The output at Q has <strong>half the frequency</strong> of the clock input.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">The 2-Bit Counter</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Two D-type flip-flops connected in series form a 2-bit counter. The Q output of the first flip-flop (Q0) clocks the second flip-flop. Both flip-flops have their \(\overline{Q}\) connected back to their own D input.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">The result: Q0 toggles on every rising edge of the external clock (CLK). Q1 toggles on every rising edge of Q0. So:</p>

<table data-narration-id="n20">
  <thead><tr><th>Clock count</th><th>Q1</th><th>Q0</th><th>Binary value</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0 (reset)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p data-narration-id="n21">The counter cycles through 0, 1, 2, 3, then resets to 0 &mdash; a modulo-4 count. This is a 2-bit binary up-counter. Q0 is the least significant bit (LSB); Q1 is the most significant bit (MSB).</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Frequency Division: Worked Numerical Example</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n22">A clock with a frequency of 4 Hz feeds a 2-bit counter made from two D-type flip-flops.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n23">First flip-flop (Q0): toggles at every rising clock edge. Frequency of Q0 = \(4 / 2 = 2\) Hz.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n24">Second flip-flop (Q1): toggles at every rising edge of Q0. Frequency of Q1 = \(2 / 2 = 1\) Hz.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n25">The pattern: each stage divides the previous frequency by 2. If Q0 is connected to an LED, that LED flashes at 2 Hz. If Q1 is connected to an LED, it flashes at 1 Hz &mdash; half the rate of Q0&rsquo;s LED. This frequency-halving at each stage is the key fact examiners test.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n26">If the exam asks for the expected frequency of Q0 and Q1 from a 12 Hz clock:
    <br>Q0 = 12 / 2 = 6 Hz
    <br>Q1 = 6 / 2 = 3 Hz</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Reading a 2-Bit Counter Timing Diagram</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n27">A 2-bit counter timing diagram shows four waveforms stacked vertically: CLK (external clock), Q0 (fastest, half CLK frequency), Q1 (slowest, half Q0 frequency).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n28">CLK: a square wave toggling continuously. Q0: toggles at each rising edge of CLK &mdash; its period is twice that of CLK. Q1: toggles at each rising edge of Q0 &mdash; its period is twice that of Q0 (four times CLK).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n29">To check: count CLK pulses between rising edges of Q1. There should be exactly 4 CLK pulses per Q1 cycle. If you see 4, you have drawn the diagram correctly. Common error: drawing Q1 toggling on Q0&rsquo;s falling edge instead of its rising edge.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n31">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n32">A D-type flip-flop copies D to Q only at the rising clock edge; between edges Q holds its stored value. \(\overline{Q}\) is always the opposite of Q.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n33">Connecting \(\overline{Q}\) back to D creates a 1-bit binary counter that divides the clock frequency by 2.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n34">Two such flip-flops in series form a 2-bit counter: Q0 runs at CLK&divide;2 and Q1 at CLK&divide;4; the counter sequences through 0, 1, 2, 3 before repeating.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p>The 2-bit counter is one of the most poorly answered topics. Drill these two facts: (1) each D-type stage divides the frequency by 2; (2) the second flip-flop clocks from Q0, not from the external clock. If a question asks for the LED flash frequencies, state Q0 = CLK&divide;2 and Q1 = CLK&divide;4 clearly before substituting numbers.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
