 },
 {
  "text": "Describe how the ABS braking system in a car uses a microcontroller to prevent wheel lock-up during heavy braking.",
  "type": "4 marks — Describe Circuit Operation",
  "marks": "Wheel-speed sensors on each wheel send digital signals to the microcontroller [1 mark]; the microcontroller continuously compares wheel speeds during braking [1 mark]; if one wheel decelerates much faster than the others, indicating lock-up, the microcontroller pulses the brake hydraulics at that wheel [1 mark]; this releases and re-applies braking approximately 15 times per second to maintain rotation and steering control [1 mark]."
 },
 {
  "text": "A microcontroller is used to control a greenhouse irrigation system. The specification states that a water pump should run for 30 seconds whenever the soil moisture sensor detects that the soil is dry (sensor output = LOW). After running, the pump must stay off for at least 60 seconds before the soil is checked again. Evaluate a candidate flowchart that correctly reads the sensor and turns the pump on, but immediately loops back to check the sensor again after switching the pump off. Identify the faults, justify why they fail the specification, and describe fully the modifications required.",
  "type": "8 marks — Extended Response: Circuit Analysis and Evaluation",
  "marks": "Mastering: Identifies two distinct faults: (1) missing 30-second run-time delay (pump switches off immediately, soil not watered adequately); (2) missing 60-second off-time delay (sensor re-checked instantly, failing the minimum off-time requirement). Justifies each fault by linking back to the exact specification clause. Describes precise modifications: add a process block 'Wait 30 000 ms' after the 'Turn pump ON' output block; add a process block 'Wait 60 000 ms' after the 'Turn pump OFF' output block before the arrow returns to the sensor-check decision. Flowchart loop structure correctly described. Notes that without these changes the pump could cycle on and off continuously, waterlogging the soil.\nSecure: Identifies both faults and describes the modifications needed, though the position of one modification block may be imprecisely stated. Links faults to specification.\nDeveloping: Identifies one fault with a correct modification; the second fault is missed or only vaguely described.\nEmerging: Recognises something is wrong with timing but cannot locate the fault precisely or describe the modification."
 }
